2026-06-30 15:49:53,359 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3680ea0a-22d5-461d-b805-e7fbf136ffd2', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_IGG1.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_IGG2.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_IGG3.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_RNase_1.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_RNase_2.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_RNase_3.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_TLR7_1.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_TLR7_2.raw\n20220311_Exploris1_SA_ISB_JLext_StrepIP_MEL562_TLR7_3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCell-Surface RNA Forms Ternary Complex with RNA-Binding Protein and Heparan Sulfate to Recruit Immune Receptors Complex with RNA-Binding Protein and Heparan Sulfate to Recruit Immune Receptor\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nRecent discoveries have shown the presence of RNA molecules on the cell surface, defying the traditional view that RNA only functions intracellularly. However, it is not well understood how cell-surface RNA (csRNA) is stably present on the plasma membrane and what functions it performs. We answer the pressing questions in the emerging field by taking integrated omic-wide approaches and multiple orthogonal validatory methods. Firstly, we exploited the RNA-sensing ability of TLR7 as a specific recombinant probe to detect csRNA. Coupling it with a genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9- knockout screening, we identified heparan sulfate (HS) as a crucial factor for RNA presentation on cells. Using the TLR7 probe, cell surface proximity labelling revealed that these HS-associated csRNAs (hepRNAs) are in vicinity with a plethora of RNA-binding proteins. The compelling observation led us to a molecular model where HS, RNA and RBP form ternary complexes at cell surface. A novel photochemical RNA-protein crosslinking technology termed SCOOPS were then established to validate the termolecular model in a TLR7-orthogonal manner. Moreover, enabled by SCOOPS, we unveiled identities of hepRNA using next-generation sequencing, and identified traits in RNA primary structures that facilitate HS association. We further show that hepRNA binds to killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptor 2DL5 (KIR2DL5), recruiting the protein to cell surface and potentially enhancing receptor-ligand interactions. Our findings provide a foundation for exploring how cell-surface ribonucleoproteins contribute to immune modulation.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe procedure follows the reported one by Santos-Barriopedro et al.59 The supernatant from the sonicated lysate of proximity biotinylated cells was subjected to bead enrichment with 12.5\u2009μL per sample streptavidin sepharose high-performance beads (Cytiva, 15511301) on an end-to-end rotator for 2\u2009h at RT. The beads were washed 5 times with 500 μL RIPA buffer and 4 times with 500 μL PBS. Beads were incubated with 50\u2009μL elution buffer (2\u2009M Urea, 10\u2009mM DTT, 100\u2009mM Tris pH 8) on a thermoshaker at 1250\u2009rpm for 20\u2009minutes at room temperature. Iodoacetamide (50\u2009mM final concentration) was then added, and the samples were incubated in a thermoshaker at 1250\u2009rpm in dark for 10\u2009minutes at room temperature. 2.5\u2009μL of trypsin (0.1\u2009mg/mL stock solution) was then added, followed by incubation in a thermoshaker at 1250\u2009rpm for 2\u2009hours at room temperature. Samples were spun down (1500 x g, 2 min, RT) and supernatant was collected. Another 50\u2009μL elution buffer was then added to the beads, which were incubated on a thermoshaker at 1250\u2009rpm for 5\u2009minutes at room temperature. Beads were then spun down (1500 x g, 2 min, RT), and the supernatant collected and combined with the first elution. 1\u2009μL trypsin (0.1\u2009mg/mL) was added and the samples were incubated overnight at room temperature. Tryptic peptides were acidified by adding 10\u2009μL 10% (v/v) TFA, and purified using C18 Stagetips (3M Empore). Briefly, StageTips were prepared and washed with methanol followed by a wash with buffer B (80% acetonitrile and 0.1% formic acid) and two washes with buffer A (0.1% formic acid). All washes were done at 1500 ×\u2009g for 4 min at RT. Samples were loaded onto the StageTips and centrifuged at 600 ×\u2009g for 10 min at RT and washed once with buffer A. StageTips were stored at 4oC.Peptides  were eluted from StageTips with 30 ml of buffer B, reduced to a volume of 5 ml by Speedvac centrifugation, after which 7 ml of buffer A (0.1% formic acid) was added. For the TLF7 proximity labeling experiments, 6 µl of each sample was loaded on an Easy LC1000 (Thermo Scientific) connected to an Orbitrap Exploris 480 (Thermo Scientific). The machine was operated in data-dependent acquisition (DDA) mode, with acquisition time set at 60 minutes. An acetonitrile gradient of 12-30% in 43 minutes was used followed by an increase of the acetonitrile concentration to 60% in 10 minutes and to 95% in 1 minute. The full scan of the peptides was set to a resolution of 120,000 in a scan range of 350–1300m/z. For biotinylation-OOPS experiments, 3 µl of each sample was loaded on a Vanquish Neo UHPLC connected to a Orbitrap Astral. The machine was operated in data-independent acquisition (DIA) mode, with acquisition time set at 24 minutes. An acetonitrile gradient of 8-35% in 15 minutes was used followed by an increase of the acetonitrile concentration to 45% in 3 minutes and to 99% in 30 seconds. The full scan of the peptides was set to a resolution of 240,000 in a scan range of 150–2000m/z.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
